#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162014 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84207049-12f7-514c-bfa2-51bce8631b41" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Projection Matrix That Orthogonally Projects a Vector to the Subspace Spanned by an Eigenvector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1556dc1b-0c8b-54e0-a340-18371f592ed3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407520} {:answer/id #uuid "286d06a8-1050-59ed-a665-558d840c2aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407525} {:answer/id #uuid "d6effcc8-e643-5b20-9090-b3855faacab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407541} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f4f75f-447a-5e0c-a1d6-feae985a30a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407543} {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93a6cf9-78b6-546d-ba88-361fc48a056a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407544} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebade115-5b0c-52d7-94cc-8ec9e5babad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11bdef24-ff68-513b-bfa6-5f2fb8c78b02" :question/math-academy-id "q-94768" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f090535-e10a-5c59-9a51-2d2aae1cbc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} {:answer/id #uuid "3f43beb7-1a8e-5d63-bf78-07894e8c2c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407527} {:answer/id #uuid "5613d7c8-bedb-54d2-be91-7e80b596eb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407528} {:answer/id #uuid "694df5b3-0ed3-5486-8f0d-63bf6d2b9e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407530} {:answer/id #uuid "bc550768-52c7-5626-83cb-470760bd86c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f090535-e10a-5c59-9a51-2d2aae1cbc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407523} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49b921e-a620-50dc-993e-f6b1a37ca3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b502656e-ef35-5f6c-921c-ae3a8ea1f2a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-95721" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}((-1)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+(-6)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-25 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162015 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785b09aa-1b82-5021-8b10-271a063cb916" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Spectral Decomposition of a Symmetric Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16877fa0-d3a3-5da8-ba77-c7e71244a7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407521} {:answer/id #uuid "6d264661-257d-5c8a-8684-122b5c0a6807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} {:answer/id #uuid "9555f6b0-dd96-561e-b956-a28b10613e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407535} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0b779d-af50-58f8-97aa-dd75027108dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407536} {:answer/id #uuid "d6231cbd-de79-555a-b056-b8008595ed25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d264661-257d-5c8a-8684-122b5c0a6807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407531} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1092e23-b9a3-5556-b0d1-0745f95be4b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e3f233e-913d-50cf-8912-1c6832afca12" :question/math-academy-id "q-95725" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}+(-9)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-65 & 78 \\\\ 78 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
